======================================================================================================================
k_cross-scale_bridge -- CANDIDATE 1: is B(R) = nu(e)[1+L(e)/3] one number shared by four system classes?
======================================================================================================================

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A.  DO THE FOUR OBSERVABLES MEASURE THE SAME FUNCTION?  This is decided by the theory, not the data.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  at the registered solar-circle field x_ext = 1.89929 a_0:  e = 1.28903, nu(e) = 1.47342, L(e) = -0.26875

  observable                                          what QUMOND actually predicts          value
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  wide binary / open cluster / cloud (g_int -> 0)     B = nu(e)[1 + L(e)/3]                 1.3414
  the same, if one wrongly drops the [1+L/3] trace    nu(e)                                 1.4734
  wide binary, FULL two-body solve (frozen band, canonical)gamma_v^2, registered                 1.3723
  wide binary, FULL two-body solve (frozen band, alt) gamma_v^2, registered                 1.4625

  AND THE FOURTH RUNG IS NOT THE SAME OBJECT AT ALL.  The vertical force K_z is generated by the Milky Way
  disc ITSELF.  The disc is not a small system embedded in someone else's field -- it IS the field.  Its
  boost is therefore nu evaluated at its OWN total Newtonian field, nu(|g_N|/a_0), with no [1+L/3] factor
  and at a DIFFERENT argument (the total field, not the in-plane circular one).  From the committed h34
  run at R_0 that number is nu = 1.444, against B = 1.3414: they differ by 8%.
  [PASS] the [1+L/3] trace factor is NOT negligible -- dropping it changes the predicted boost by more than 5% [CAN FAIL]   (nu(e) = 1.4734 vs B = 1.3414, a factor 1.0984 (9.8%))
  [PASS] THE UNIVERSALITY CLAIM FAILS IN THE THEORY: the four observables are NOT one number.  The vertical-force rung predicts nu(y_local) = 1.444 while the embedded-system rungs predict B = 1.341, and the full two-body wide-binary solve predicts a third value  [CAN FAIL -- this is the candidate's own claim]   (B = 1.3414, two-body gamma_v^2 = 1.3723 (+2.3%), vertical force nu = 1.444 (+7.6%) -- a spread of 8% before a single measurement)
  So 'the same B measured four ways' is not a law the theory states.  The theory states THREE different
  numbers spanning 7.7%, and the law as proposed would be falsified by the theory's own arithmetic.
  What the theory DOES state is weaker and still worth writing down: all FOUR boosts are set by the
  EXTERNAL/local field and none of them by the system's own acceleration.  That is section B's test.

