  [PASS] the restatement test does NOT close: the BTFR boost sqrt(a_0/g_int) varies by more than a factor 3 across these classes while B varies by 0%  [CAN FAIL]   (BTFR boosts span 1.46-5.18, a factor 3.5; B is a single number)
  is_restatement = FALSE.  The content is the EXTERNAL-FIELD EFFECT, which is standard published MOND
  (Milgrom 1983 ApJ 270 365; Bekenstein & Milgrom 1984 ApJ 286 7; Famaey & McGaugh 2012 Living Rev. Rel. 15,
  Sec. 6.3), and nu(e)[1+L/3] is the angle-average of the KNOWN anisotropic EFE dilation, not a new object.

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT ON CANDIDATE 1
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] (1) a relation between MEASURED quantities
         yes for each rung individually
  [PASS] (2) a_0 appears with a PREDICTED coefficient
         yes -- B(e) is parameter-free once a_0 is fixed
  [FAIL] (3) holds across many systems to <= 0.1 dex
         NO.  Two rungs are measured, not four; they sit 0.53 apart in B; and once their own modelling systematics are carried each is uncertain by 0.09-0.25 in B, i.e. 0.03-0.12 dex, against a predicted signal of 0.128 dex.  The measurement is at best marginal, never 0.1 dex.
  [FAIL] (4) nobody has stated it
         the external-field effect and its dilation coefficient are long published; only the four-way assembly is new phrasing
  [PASS] (5) NOT a restatement of v^4 = G M_b a_0
         confirmed by execution in section C

  >>> 3 of 5 criteria met.  CANDIDATE 1 IS NOT A SECOND KEPLER-GRADE LAW.

  >>> THE DECISIVE FINDING IS STRUCTURAL AND NEEDED NO DATA (section A): the four observables do NOT
      measure one number.  The vertical force is generated BY the disc, so its boost is nu(y_local) = 1.444,
      not the embedded-system B = 1.341; and the frozen wide-binary band comes from a full two-body
      solve at finite internal field, 1.3723, not from the point-mass asymptote.  The claim's own
      three numbers span 7.6% in B, which is 30% of the ENTIRE
      signal against Newton (B - 1 = 0.341).  A law whose four members disagree by a third of the
      effect they are supposed to share is not a law -- and no data are needed to see it.

  >>> WHAT SURVIVES, and it is real but weaker: the EFE must be switched on.  Three independent classes now
      say so -- molecular clouds (isolated MOND out at 5.7 sigma, k03), open clusters (out at 36 sigma, this
      session), and the fact that open clusters and wide binaries do not look dark-matter dominated at all.
      That is a confirmation of standard MOND, not a new law, and it is credited above.

  >>> AGAINST INTEREST, twice.  (a) Both measured rungs sit BELOW their own framework prediction, and the
      vertical-force rung sits below Newton's 1.000 as well on the Bovy & Rix normalisation.  (b) The one
      rung the candidate calls decisive -- globular clusters at low e -- already EXCLUDES the saturation
      law at 9.5 sigma over 0.98 dex of predicted signal (k02), and that kill is on disk and is not new.

  UPSILON LEVERS, per rung, as the task requires:
    wide binaries (1e-4 pc, 1 Msun)             d log(gamma_v)/d log(Upsilon_ML) = -0.500, so d log(gamma_v - 1)/d log Upsilon ~ -3.6
    open clusters (1 pc, 1e3 Msun)              d log(M_dyn/M_phot)/d log(Upsilon) = -1.000 (measured by re-running)
    molecular clouds (10 pc, 1e5 Msun)          d log(alpha_vir)/d log(Upsilon_star) = 0.000 EXACTLY; replaced by d/d log X_CO = -1.000
    vertical force (1 kpc, 1e10 Msun)           d log(K_z/K_z,bar)/d log(Upsilon) = -1.000 (Sigma_star proportional to Upsilon)
    globular clusters (10 pc, 1e5 Msun, LOW e)  d log(M/L_V)/d log(Upsilon_V) = -1.000 for the amplitude; 0.000 for the slope
    SHAPE statements (the cross-class equality, the R_gal rise) carry d/d log Upsilon = 0.000 exactly,
    because each rung's boost is a ratio of a dynamical quantity to a photometric one at fixed geometry --
    but every AMPLITUDE, which is what the law actually predicts, carries a lever of -0.5 to -1.0.

